 slug:"australian-artist-margaret-preston",path:"grade-12/reading/australian-artist-margaret-preston",title:"Australian Artist Margaret Preston",minutes:20,total:13,
 source:"https://mini-ielts.com/1518/print/reading/australian-artist-margaret-preston",
 paragraphs:[
 "Margaret Preston was born in South Australia in 1875 and later studied art in Sydney and Melbourne. Her family circumstances changed during her schooling, yet she had already begun to pursue artistic training. In the earlier part of her career she was influenced by a German approach that represented natural subjects realistically, rather than treating them as purely decorative shapes.",
 "Preston visited Europe and studied in Paris, where the available account does not identify a particular famous art college that she attended. In England she explored pottery and printmaking and also taught basket weaving to recovering servicemen. She met William Preston on a vessel travelling back to Australia, not while instructing the men at the rehabilitation centre. They married in 1919 and established a home in Sydney.",
 "During the 1920s Preston developed prints of Sydney Harbour, boats and hillside buildings. She exhibited alongside her friend Thea Proctor, but the record of shared exhibitions does not establish that both women explored the same artistic themes. Some harbour compositions from this period were compact and visually busy; that description cannot, on its own, prove that they were simpler than every earlier work.",
 "Between 1932 and 1939 Preston lived in the bushland of Berowra. Unlike the flowers previously bought from a shop, plants now grew around her home, and she turned her attention to blossoms in their natural setting. Her prints tended to become bigger and less intricate while depending less on vivid colour. The changes are specific: reduced complexity does not mean the later prints retained their former brilliance of colour.",
 "Rock engravings near her home encouraged Preston to study Indigenous Australian art. In later prints she adopted symbols and earthy colours associated with Aboriginal traditions. She frequently acknowledged these sources in the titles of her works. Such acknowledgements are evidence of inspiration; they should not be confused with claims that she originated the traditions herself.",
 "In 1953 Preston exhibited a series of significant prints, some produced through the old method of stenciling. She had collected books concerning Chinese art from 1915 and had travelled to China. Her mature art could combine Chinese design ideas with interpretations informed by Aboriginal cultural narratives.",
 "Prest​on continued painting, making prints and travelling as she grew older. Across nearly six decades of artistic work she produced more than 400 paintings and prints before her death in 1963. Her sustained commitment is separate from the question of which precise artistic influence appeared in any particular decade."
 ],
